G3195.d:
μέλλω, 
 [in LXX: Job.3:8 (עָתִיד) 19:25 (אַחֲרוֹן); elsewhere for fut., and frequently in Wi, II, 4Mac ;] 
to be about to be or do; 
  1. with inf. (Bl., §62, 4; 68, 2; M, Pr., 114); 
  (a) of intending or being about to do of  one's own free will: with inf. praes., Matt.2:13, Luke.10:1, Acts.3:3 5:35, Heb.8:5, 2 Pet.1:12 (Field, Notes, 240), al.; with inf. aor. (Bl., §58, 3), Acts.12:6, Rev.3:16; 
  (b) of compulsion, necessity or certainty: with inf. praes., Matt.16:27, Luke.9:31, John.6:71, Rom.4:24, al.; with inf. aor., Rom.8:18, Gal.3:23, Rev.3:2 12:4. 
  2. Ptcp., ὁ μέλλων: absol., Rom.8:38, 1 Cor.3:22; τὰ μ., Col.2:17; εἰς τὸ μ. (Field, Notes, 65); with subst., Matt.3:7 12:32 (ὁ αἰὼν ὁ μ.; LXX for עַד), Acts.24:25, 1 Tim.4:8, Heb.2:5, al. 
 (AS)

G3307.d:
μερίζω
 (μέρος), [in LXX chiefly for חָלַק ;] 
to divide; 
  (a) to divide into parts : metaphorically, pass., 1 Cor.7:34 (WH, R, ing.), 1 Cor.7:34 (Rec., R, txt.) ( reading and punctuation, see ICC, in l); μεμέρισται ὁ Χριστός, 1 Cor.1:13; as in late authors, of factional division (cf. Polyb., viii, 23, 9), καθ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ, Matt.12:25; ἐφ᾽ εαυτόν, Matt.12:26, Mark.3:24-26; 
  (b) to distribute: with accusative of thing(s) and dative of person(s), Mark.6:41; as in later usage (cf. Polyb., xi, 28, 9), to bestow: Rom.12:3, 1 Cor.7:17, 2 Cor.10:13, Heb.7:2; mid., with accusative of thing(s) before μετά, Luke.12:13 (cf. δια-, συμ-μερίζω).†
 (AS)

G3360.d:
μέχρι
 (bef. consonants, exc. Luke.16:16, μέχρι Ἰωάνου) and μέχρις (bef. vowels, Mk, Ga, ll. with, Heb.12:4; see Bl., § 5, 4) 
  1. as prep., with genitive, as far as, even to, until; 
  (a) of place: Rom.15:19; 
  (b) of time : Matt.11:23 13:30 28:15, Luke.16:16, Acts.10:30 20:7, Rom.5:14, 1 Tim.6:14, Heb.3:6, 14 9:10; 
  (with) of measure or degree : Phil.2:8, 30, 2 Tim.2:9, Heb.12:4 (μ. αἵματος, cf. 2Ma.13:14). 
  2. As conjunct. (as long as), until: Eph.4:13; μ. οὗ (Thuc., iii, 28, but more frequently μ. ἄν see BI., § 65, 10), Mark.13:30, Gal.4:19 (μ. is prop, an adv., cf. Lat. usgue, before prep. or adv.; LS, see word, and cf. ἄχρι).†
 (AS)

G3391.d:
μία, see: εἷς.
  εἷς 
 ιά, ἕν, genitive ἑνός, μιᾶς, ἑνός, 
 cardinal numeral, one; 
  1. one, as opposite to many: Matt.25:15, Rom.5:12, 1 Cor.10:8, al.; as subst., Rom.5:15, Eph.2:14; id. with genitive partit., Matt.5:19, al.; before ἐκ (ἐξ), Mark.14:18, John.6:8, al.; metaphorically, of union and concord, John.10:30 17:11, Rom.12:4-5, Phil.1:27; ἀπὸ μιᾶς (B1., § 44, 1), Luke.14:18; with neg., εἷς. . . οὐ (μή), more emphatic than οὐδείς, no one, none (cl.), Matt.5:18 10:29, Luke.11:46 12:6. 
  2. Emphatically, to the exclusion of others; 
  (a) a single (one): Matt.21:24, Mark.8:14; absol., 1 Cor.9:24, al.; οὐδὲ εἷς, Matt.27:14, John.1:3, Rom.3:10, al.; 
  (b) one, alone: Mark.2:7 10:18, Luke.18:19; 
  (with) one and the same: Rom.3:30, 1 Cor.3:8 11:5 12:11, 1 John.5:8. 
  3. In late Gk., with weakened force, = τις or indef. art. (of. Heb. אֶחָד, Gen.22:13, al.; see B1., § 45, 2; M, Pr., 96 f.): Matt.8:19 19:6, Rev.8:13, al.; εἷς τις (Bl., l.with), Luke.22:50, John.11:49 
  4. Distributively: εἷς καστος (cl.), Luke.4:40, Acts.2:6, al.; εἷς . . . καὶ εἷς (cl., εἷς μὲν . . . εἷς δέ), Matt.17:4, Mark.9:5, John.20:12, al. (cf. LXX and use of Heb. אֶחָד, Exod.17:12, al); ὁ εἷς . . . ὁ ἕτερος (ἄλλος) = cl. ὁ μὲν (ἕτερος) . . . ὁ δέ (ἕτερος), Matt.6:24, Luke.7:41, Rev.17:10; καθ᾽ εἷς, εἷς κ. είς (in which καθ᾽ is adverbial, or the expression formed from the analogy of ἕν καθ᾽ ἔν; M, Pr., 105), one by one, severally: Mark.14:19, Rom.12:5, al.; εἷς τὸν ἕνα = ἀλλήλους (B1., 45, 2; M, Pr., 246), 1 Thess.5:11. 
  5. As ordinal = πρῶτος (like Heb. אֶחָד; Bl., § 45, 1; M, Pr., 95 f.), first: Matt.28:1, Mark.16:2, al 
 (AS)

G3392.d:
μιαίνω 
 [in LXX chiefly for טָמֵא ;] 
  1. to dye or stain. 
  2. to stain, defile, soil; 
  (a) in physical sense; 
  (b) in moral sense : Titus.1:15, Heb.12:15, Ju 8; 
  (with) in ritual sense (cf. Lev.22:5, al.): John.18:28.†
 SYN.: μολύνω, to besmear, which also differs from μ. in that it is never used, as μ. in its primary meaning, in an honourable sense (cf. Tr., Syn., § xxxi)  (AS)

G3398.d:
μικρός, -ά, -όν 
 [in LXX chiefly for קָטָן, מָעַט ;] 
small, little; 
  1. of persons; 
  (a) lit., of stature: Mark.15:40 (MM, iii, xvi; on the view that age is meant, see Deiss., BS, 144), Luke.19:3; οἱ μ., the little ones, Matt.18:6, 10 18:14, Mark.9:42; 
  (b) hence metaphorically, of rank or influence (cf. Dalman, Words, 113 f.) : Matt.10:42, Luke.17:2, Acts.8:10 26:22, Heb.8:11" (LXX), Rev.11:18 13:16 19:5, 18 20:12; compar., -ότερος, Matt.11:11, Luke.7:28 9:48. 
  2. Of things; 
  (a) of size : Matt.13:32 Mark.4:31, Jas.3:5; 
  (b) of quantity: Luke.12:32, 1 Cor.5:6, Gal.5:9, Rev.3:8; 
  (with) of time : John.7:33 12:35, Rev.6:11 20:3. 
  3. Neut., μικρόν, used adverbially; 
  (a) of distance: Matt.26:39, Mark.14:35; 
  (b) of quantity: 2 Cor.11:1 11:16; 
  (with) of time : John.13:33 14:19 16:16-19, Heb.10:37; μετὰ μ., Matt.26:73, Mark.14:70.†
 (AS)

G3403.d:
μιμνήσκω
 (Bl., -ῄ-, § 3, 3), [in LXX chiefly for זָכַר ;] 
to remind: mid, and pass.; 
  (a) reflexive, to remind oneself of, hence, to remember: with genitive of thing(s), Matt.26:75, Luke.1:54, 72 24:8, Acts.11:16, 2 Pet.3:2, Ju 17; with neg., of sins, = to forgive, Heb.8:12 10:17" (LXX) ; with genitive of person(s), Luke.23:42; before ὅτι, Matt.5:23 27:63, Luke.16:25, John.2:17, 22 12:16; ὡς, Luke.24:6; pf., μέμνημι, in Pres. sense (cl.), with genitive of person(s) (of thing(s)), 1 Cor.11:2, 2 Tim.1:4; pres., μιμνήσκομαι (only in late writers), with genitive of person(s), in sense of caring for, Heb.2:6 (LXX) Heb.13:3; 
  (b) in passive sense, to be remembered, aor., ἐμνήσθην : before ἐνώπιον, with genitive of person(s) (cf. Eze.18:22), Acts.10:31, Rev.16:19 (cf. ἀνα-, ἐπ-ανα-, ὑπο- μιμνήσκω. The tenses of this verb are from the older μνάομαι).†
 (AS)

G3438.d:
μονή, -ῆς, ἡ
 (μένω), [in LXX: 1Ma.7:38 * ;] 
  1. in cl., 
  (a) a staying, abiding; 
  (b) continuance (LXX, 1 with) 
  2. In late Gk., 
  (a) a statson (Paus.); 
  (b) an abode: John.14:2, 23; 
  (with) a monastery (cf. MM, iii, xvi; so in MGr.).†
 (AS)

G3487.d:
νάρδος, -ου, ἡ
 (Heb. נֵרְדְּ, both from Sanscrit narda, see Boisacq, see word), [in LXX: Sng.1:12 4:13-14 (נֵרְדְּ)* ;] 
nard; 
  (a) an Indian plant, the Nardostachys nardus jatamansi, used for the preparation of a fragrant ointment; 
  (b) ointment of nard: Mark.14:3, John.12:3.†
 (AS)
